WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.130
Your tailoring business: customer, money words and safe setup.

2
00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:08.510
This is the video for module 1 of Fashion Business Management.

3
00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:17.440
Many talented tailors stay small for years. Not because their sewing is poor, but because they run the business like a hobby.

4
00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:27.480
They accept every style, quote prices from their head, mix shop money with house money, and sew late into the night before every festive season.

5
00:00:28.730 --> 00:00:36.170
In this module you choose your customer, learn the money words every tailor needs, and set up your workshop safely and properly.

6
00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:49.330
Most Nigerian tailors start with bespoke work. The client brings fabric, you measure, she pays a deposit and collects later. It needs little stock, but your income depends on your hours.

7
00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:56.210
Ready-to-wear can grow beyond your own hands, but your money is tied up in stock until it sells.

8
00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:03.040
Many small brands mix the two: bespoke pays the bills while a small ready-to-wear line builds the brand.

9
00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:13.250
For example, Chiamaka runs a two-machine shop in Owerri. She sews anything that walks in, and is always busy but rarely has savings.

10
00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:22.370
She groups her last 40 orders: 22 women's gowns and skirt suits, 8 men's styles, 6 repairs and 4 bridal pieces.

11
00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:33.610
Working women from banks and schools paid deposits without long bargaining. Bridal pieces took about three times longer and caused the most fitting disputes.

12
00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:42.640
So she focuses on working women, keeps repairs as a side service, and pauses bridal gowns until she has trained help.

13
00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:50.550
She writes: I make well-fitted office and church wear for working women in Owerri, ready in 7 to 10 days.

14
00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:53.860
Your promise must be something you can keep every time.

15
00:01:55.110 --> 00:02:01.830
Total cost has three parts. First, direct materials: everything that goes into this one garment.

16
00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:15.240
Second, labour, the most forgotten cost. If you want about 150,000 naira as your wage and you sew about 150 hours a month, your rate is 1,000 naira an hour.

17
00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:22.230
A gown that takes 8 hours carries 8,000 naira of labour. If you skip it, you are working for free.

18
00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:30.260
Third, overheads. Fuel, shop rent and data are costs of every garment, even though you pay them monthly.

19
00:02:31.510 --> 00:02:39.600
Now two words that confuse many tailors. Markup compares profit with cost. Margin compares profit with the selling price.

20
00:02:40.850 --> 00:02:47.870
Bisi sells a lined Ankara gown for 40,000 naira. Her total cost is about 30,000.

21
00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:55.920
Her profit is 10,000 naira. That is a 33% markup, but a 25% margin.

22
00:02:56.270 --> 00:03:11.140
If she wrongly adds 25% to cost, she charges only 37,500 and her margin falls to 20%. To reach a 25% margin, divide the cost by 0.75.

23
00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:21.710
Now safety. The iron alone draws a lot of power, so give it its own socket. Use a stabiliser or surge protector for electric machines.

24
00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:33.570
Never run a generator indoors, in a passage or near windows. Its exhaust contains carbon monoxide, a gas you cannot see or smell, which can kill.

25
00:03:34.820 --> 00:03:40.730
Keep fabric and fuel away from heat, and know how to use your fire extinguisher or bucket of sand.

26
00:03:41.080 --> 00:03:45.660
Also change bent needles at once, and keep scissors closed when not in use.

27
00:03:46.910 --> 00:03:54.120
Tag each client's fabric with her name, phone, order number and due date, and photograph it. That photo is your proof.

28
00:03:54.470 --> 00:04:05.030
Register your business name on the official CAC portal, never through a stranger promising a certificate in one hour. And keep measurements and phone numbers private.

29
00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:12.130
To remember. Serve a clear customer. Pay yourself inside the price. Use margin correctly.

30
00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:18.750
Keep deposits for the orders they were paid for, because sales are not profit. And keep your workshop safe.

31
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:29.450
Your turn. Review your orders and write your business statement. Cost one garment properly. Fix two risks in your workshop, and search two business names.

32
00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:31.740
Continue with the lessons below.
